Riikka Koo wrote:

> Right, certainly there are many different practices about using the
> possessives. Let's rephrase the question: is there an actual
> recommendation somewhere that the of-genitive should always be used
> with inanimate objects? That's what you seemed to say in your first
> message (and actually later, too), and that's what's been bugging me.

The answer to that question is "yes, that was what used to be
taught. But over the years, the rule has been more and more
relaxed."  When I was in high school, if I had written
"the car's door" in a term paper, it would have cost me
points.  10 years later, it would not have.  The rule is
still taught as a guideline, because "the car's door" sounds
stilted.  Usually inanimate objects taking the possessive
sounds wrong, but under certain circumstances, it actually
sounds better than saying "the edge of the water" and 
clearer than "the water edge".  


> But I'm afraid you missed my point. What I was trying to say was
> that you cannot say that the language is flexible just because there
> is a rule that doesn't need to be followed. 

There are new versions of English evolving all the time.
Along the rivers in Africa where pidgins English emerged
as a trading language, these pidgins have evolved into
creoles.  

Recently, in the southern black version of English, where it
was once thought that "I be, he be, they be," etc was
just an ignorant way to (fail to) conjugate "to be",
it turns out that this was a hold over from African
languages which had a particle "d'"  

"I d' go"

"He d' go"

etc.

The d'  evolved to "be".  

And ebonics emerged as a dialect in the inner cities.

So there's three examples of new English dialects emerging
and feeding the main branch of English.  Any one of these
could end up being the main branch.  These dialects have
grammars quite different from ordinary English.

English tolerates these things.  That's another type
of flexibility.


